# Idea Compounding (Build On, Don't Only Kill)

**STANDING DIRECTIVE (Tristan 2026-07-30):** Ideas are the currency. A bad idea, a terrible idea, an awful idea, and a drug-induced idea — brought together — can become a genius idea nobody had alone. Agents must **compound** ideas, not only refute them.

## The bar (pass/fail)

| Must be TRUE | Must be FALSE |
|---|---|
| Every serious proposal gets at least one **additive** move: keep the useful fragment + attach something that makes it stronger | Only a kill table / “won’t work because…” with no next construction |
| Kills name the **failure mode**, then ask “what version survives that mode?” | Treating a kill as the end of the thread |
| Competing ideas are stacked (A’s constraint + B’s process + C’s geometry) before one is crowned | Demanding each idea be genius in isolation |
| Half-baked ideas stay on the table long enough to be improved | Politeness-kill or premature elegance filter |

## Protocol (every non-trivial proposal)

When Tony / Tristan / a council / a paper / a patent drops an idea:

1. **Steelman the fragment** — what physical or economic truth is it pointing at? (one sentence)
2. **Name the real failure mode** — not vibe; the mechanism that breaks (adhesive loss, Ra vs skin depth, pitch growth, cost, …)
3. **Compound** — add at least one of:
   - a **constraint from another thread** (actuator pitch, Vlad Floquet, cost/target life)
   - a **process inversion** (form onto a master instead of free-form)
   - a **stack** (face / body / backplane; structure metal + RF skin)
   - a **yield strategy** (tile, bin, reject, calibrate)
4. **Return a better version** — “X + Y → Z” with the open question that would prove Z
5. **Only then** rank Live / Research / Parked / Killed — and a Kill must cite the failure mode *and* what fragment to reuse elsewhere

## Anti-patterns

| Anti-pattern | Why it fails Tristan’s philosophy |
|---|---|
| Instant kill with no salvage | Burns currency; the fragment dies with the wrapper |
| “Not our scope” as a thought-stopper | Scope can park work; it must not erase the idea |
| Demanding a complete solution before allowing the idea | Genius is usually a *stack* of incomplete ideas |
| Red-team-only posture | Red team finds holes; blue team must fill them with better geometry |

## Relationship to other rules

- **CORE FIX PRINCIPLE** — still fix the SOURCE rule in code; this rule governs *how we treat human/engineering proposals* before and while encoding them.
- **Gate intent** — a kill without a routed next construction is a walk-through gate on creativity.
- **Work quality** — compounding is not scope creep; the additive move should be the *smallest* attachment that changes the verdict from dead → live-research.

## Quick checklist

- [ ] Stated the useful fragment
- [ ] Stated the failure mode
- [ ] Offered at least one compound (X + Y → Z)
- [ ] Named the prove-it question
- [ ] Verdict is Live / Research / Parked / Killed — never “ignored”
